Yes, loops are used to repeat a block of code a certain number of times.  There are two types of loops:  infinite loops and non-infinite loops.  An infinite loop will continue to execute the code inside the loop until it reaches a certain condition, like the number of times it’s been executed.  Non-infinite loops will only execute the code inside the loop the specified number of times.  For example, the code below will execute the code inside the loop 5 times, and then stop.  The code below will execute the code inside the loop 10 times, and then stop.

for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    System.out.println("Hello, world!");
}

The "i" variable inside the loop represents the number of times the code has executed.  It starts out at 0 and increments by 1 each time the loop runs.  The "i < 10" condition checks whether the value of "i" is less than 10.  If it is, the code inside the loop will execute.  If it’s not, the loop will stop.  So, this loop will execute 10 times, and then stop.

